## The problem

A merchant with volume does not use one courier. They use several — one that is
cheap for metro deliveries, one that actually reaches tier-3 cities, one whose
COD remittance is fastest — and often several accounts with the same carrier for
different business units.

Every one of those carriers exposes a different API. They disagree on how to
identify a shipment, what statuses exist, whether weight is grams or kilograms,
how to express a serviceable pincode, and what a failed delivery means. Some
return XML. Some return HTTP 200 with an error in the body.

The naive integration — a branch per carrier scattered through the order
handling code — works for two carriers and becomes unmaintainable at four,
because the business logic and the carrier quirks are interleaved and cannot be
tested apart.

## Architecture

One internal domain model, and an adapter per carrier that translates in both
directions. The rest of the application never learns that carriers differ.

```ts
// Every carrier implements this. The application only knows this interface.
export interface CarrierAdapter {
  readonly id: CarrierId;

  quote(shipment: Shipment): Promise<Quote[]>;
  book(shipment: Shipment, account: CarrierAccount): Promise<Booking>;
  track(awb: string, account: CarrierAccount): Promise<TrackingEvent[]>;
  cancel(awb: string, account: CarrierAccount): Promise<void>;

  /** Carrier status strings → our fixed status enum. */
  normaliseStatus(raw: string): ShipmentStatus;
}
```

`normaliseStatus` looks trivial and was the highest-value part of the design.
Carriers between them expose dozens of status strings — `IN TRANSIT`,
`Intransit`, `SHIPMENT_PICKED`, `RTO_INITIATED` — and the moment any of those
leak into business logic, every downstream feature has to know every carrier's
vocabulary. Collapsing them at the boundary into a small fixed enum meant
reporting, notifications, and the merchant UI were written once.

**Idempotency, because shipping is not a read.** Booking a shipment costs money
and cannot be undone cheaply. Network timeouts are common with carrier APIs, and
a timeout does not tell you whether the booking happened. Every mutating
operation carries a client-generated idempotency key, persisted before the call
and reconciled after, so a retry after an ambiguous failure either returns the
original booking or completes it — never books twice.

**Everything carrier-facing runs on a queue.** Carrier APIs are slow and
unreliable, and neither property should be visible to a merchant clicking
"ship". Bookings, tracking refreshes, and label generation run as BullMQ jobs
with per-carrier concurrency limits and exponential backoff. Labels land in S3;
the record stores a key rather than a blob.

**Reporting reads from a projection.** Analytics over live order tables meant
slow dashboards and lock contention with order processing. Shipment events feed
a denormalised reporting table written by the same workers, so reads never
compete with the write path.

## Challenges

**Carriers change without telling you.** A response field silently became a
string. A status appeared that no documentation mentioned. The adapter layer
absorbed these, and unknown statuses now map to an explicit `UNKNOWN` that
alerts rather than crashing — a shipment in an unrecognised state should page
someone, not throw a 500 at a merchant.

**Multi-account within one carrier.** Credentials, rate limits, and serviceable
pincodes are per-account, not per-carrier. Modelling the account as a first-class
entity rather than a config blob on the carrier made rate limiting and
per-business-unit reporting fall out naturally. I got this wrong first and
retrofitted it, which cost about a week.

**Reconciliation.** Carrier invoices do not match what the system thinks was
shipped — weight discrepancies, surcharges applied later, RTO charges appearing
weeks after. Invoicing had to model the difference between *expected* and
*settled* cost instead of assuming the quote was final.

## Outcome

A working logistics platform handling order processing, multi-account shipping
across carriers, invoicing with reconciliation, and reporting — with adding a new
carrier reduced to implementing one interface rather than touching the order
pipeline.

The general lesson is one I now apply reflexively: when integrating systems you
do not control, spend the time on the boundary. An adapter layer feels like
ceremony when you have one integration. At four it is the only reason the
codebase is still comprehensible.
